-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5954+) **AI unit upgrades - stop routine upgrades from draining the treasury:** Fixes an AI spending problem where normal unit **upgrades could overshoot the intended upgrade budget**. Previously, the AI only checked whether it was still below budget before starting the next upgrade, but did not check whether that next upgrade itself fit the remaining budget. For example, in BBAI diagnostics, an AI with 280 gold reserved for upgrades could spend 237 gold, still count as under budget, then start another 237 gold upgrade and end up spending 474 gold total, sometimes leaving only a few gold in the treasury. Routine **upgrades now check the actual remaining upgrade budget before upgrading**. **Emergency**-style upgrades, such as important city defenders or special impassable-unit upgrades, **remain more flexible**, but normal upgrades are much less likely to waste all available gold. AIs in **financial trouble also avoid** routine upgrades **unless they are focused on war**. See [KI#160](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#160---fixed-likely-inherited-ai-upgrade-budget-issue-normal-upgrades-could-overshoot-the-remaining-budget-and-leave-the-ai-almost-broke).
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5952+) **AI attack stacks now opportunistically capture nearby barbarian cities:** before this fix, ready city-attack-capable (i.e. not just `UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY`) stacks could sit idle because the AI had **no normal war target yet**, even with a **nearby pathable barbarian city**. Now, if the AI has **no normal target** and is **not already busy with an active war**, the stack can **attack that barbarian city instead**. This does not override normal wars or normal war targets; it fills the no-target idle gap. This change is usually a **strong** move: barbarian cities are often weak, cost little diplomatically, and capturing them can give land, population, resources, unit support, and future growth without spending a settler or interrupting city production nor growth. In the tested save before the fix, nearby barbarian cities survived at game end at ~T400 despite reachable AI armies to Napoleon and Shaka, and barbarian being very weak in comparison, yet bonuses-rich, and Shaka was among weakest players; after the fix, Zulu captured Hurrian at T127, Alemanni at T132, Visigoth at T148, and Gaul at T153, turning those nearby barb cities into an early expansion boost and making Shaka the strongest player for almost all middle game and end game, almost allowing him to win. This change should make AI significantly stronger. See [KI#158](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#158---fixed-base-advciv-issue-ready-no-target-attack-stacks-could-ignore-pathable-barbarian-cities-while-only-preparing-a-future-war).
